    "created_at": "2017-01-10T16:53:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-01-10T16:54:23Z",
    "body": "Thanks, @zcorpan:\r\n\r\n> Just descendant of form is not sufficient. Controls can be associated with a form without being descendants. Is the problem just in select? I guess datalist could also be a problem, but less so. \r\n\r\n\"contained in\" was the wrong term, I agree. \"conceptually contained in\"? :) We'd certainly want to take care of things like `<select form>`, `<textarea form>`, `<button form>`, etc (`<option>` is not the only element that eats subsequent elements).\r\n\r\n> Another option could be to change the parser to ignore plaintext in select, although there is resistance against changing the parser in general...\r\n\r\nI don't think we'd need to change the parser to ignore `<plaintext>` (and doing so would break the xss mitigations like Safen Up that I noted above). We could change the way we gather form data, however, perhaps poking somewhere around step 3.1 of https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#constructing-the-form-data-set to skip over an element if it contains `<plaintext>` if that turns out to be simpler than blocking the submission altogether.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
